  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)}{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
